Kube-OVN security groups — what was read, where, and on what date.

⚠ EVERY FACT BELOW COMES FROM THE GO TYPES OR THE CONTROLLER, NOT FROM THE DOCUMENTATION SITE.
charts/managed/kube-ovn-vpc/SOURCE records why. Read on 2026-08-18 against `master`.

  https://github.com/kubeovn/kube-ovn
    pkg/apis/kubeovn/v1/security-group.go
    pkg/controller/security_group.go
    pkg/ovs/ovn-nb-acl.go
    pkg/util/const.go

── Scope and naming ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    // +genclient
    // +genclient:nonNamespaced
    // +resourceName=security-groups
    // +kubebuilder:resource:scope="Cluster",shortName="sg",path="security-groups",singular="security-group"
    // +kubebuilder:subresource:status

⚠ THE PLURAL IS `security-groups`, HYPHENATED. ClusterConformanceHarness derives its CRD stub's path
from GroupVersionKind.Plural, so a guessed `securitygroups` installs a definition at a path the apply
never reaches — and the symptom is a discovery error naming a missing operator.

── The spec ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    type SecurityGroupSpec struct {
        IngressRules          []SecurityGroupRule `json:"ingressRules,omitempty"`
        EgressRules           []SecurityGroupRule `json:"egressRules,omitempty"`
        AllowSameGroupTraffic bool                `json:"allowSameGroupTraffic,omitempty"`
        Tier                  int                 `json:"tier,omitempty"`
    }

    type SecurityGroupRule struct {
        IPVersion           string       `json:"ipVersion"`
        Protocol            SgProtocol   `json:"protocol,omitempty"`
        Priority            int          `json:"priority,omitempty"`
        RemoteType          SgRemoteType `json:"remoteType"`
        RemoteAddress       string       `json:"remoteAddress,omitempty"`
        RemoteSecurityGroup string       `json:"remoteSecurityGroup,omitempty"`
        PortRangeMin        int          `json:"portRangeMin,omitempty"`
        PortRangeMax        int          `json:"portRangeMax,omitempty"`
        LocalAddress        string       `json:"localAddress,omitempty"`
        SourcePortRangeMin  int          `json:"sourcePortRangeMin,omitempty"`
        SourcePortRangeMax  int          `json:"sourcePortRangeMax,omitempty"`
        Policy              SgPolicy     `json:"policy"`
    }

⚠ `ipVersion` IS `ipv4` / `ipv6`, LOWER CASE — the OPPOSITE spelling to `Subnet.spec.protocol`'s
`IPv4`/`IPv6`/`Dual`, in the same API group, on an adjacent object. validateSgRule:

    if rule.IPVersion != "ipv4" && rule.IPVersion != "ipv6" {
        return errors.New("IPVersion should be 'ipv4' or 'ipv6'")
    }

⚠ `policy` IS `allow` / `drop` / `pass`, NOT `deny`. The doc comment in the Go source says "allow,
pass or deny" and the constants are SgPolicyAllow / SgPolicyDrop / SgPolicyPass bound to OVN ACL
actions. A third README-versus-code disagreement for this tree.

Protocol constants: SgProtocolALL "all", SgProtocolICMP "icmp", SgProtocolTCP "tcp", SgProtocolUDP
"udp". Remote types: SgRemoteTypeAddress "address", SgRemoteTypeSg "securityGroup".

⚠ ZERO `+kubebuilder:default`, ZERO `required:` and ZERO `enum:` in this CRD's schema — the same as
Subnet, IptablesEIP and OvnEip. Client-side validation is the only validation before the controller.

── Default deny: established rather than assumed ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

THIS IS THE FINDING THIS TYPE MOST NEEDED. pkg/ovs/ovn-nb-acl.go:

  CreateSgDenyAllACL   installs "outport == @{pgName} && ip" and "inport == @{pgName} && ip" with
                       action DROP, at util.SecurityGroupDropPriority
  CreateSgBaseACL      adds ARP, ICMPv6, DHCP and VRRP exceptions at SecurityGroupBasePriority
  UpdateSgACL          clears one direction, then writes same-group and per-rule ACLs
  newSgRuleACL         one ACL per rule, at SecurityGroupAllowPriority

pkg/util/const.go:

  SecurityGroupBasePriority   = "2005"
  SecurityGroupAllowPriority  = "2004"
  SecurityGroupDropPriority   = "2003"
  SecurityGroupHighestPriority = "18484"
  SecurityGroupPriorityMax    = 16384
  SecurityGroupPriorityMin    = 1
  SecurityGroupOvnTierBase    = 2
  SecurityGroupAPITierMinimum = 0
  SecurityGroupAPITierMaximum = 1
  DenyAllSecurityGroup        = "kubeovn_deny_all"
  SecurityGroupAnnotationTemplate = "%s.kubernetes.io/security_groups"

⚠ SO UNMATCHED TRAFFIC IS DROPPED, AND AN EMPTY RULE SET PERMITS NOTHING. pkg/controller/
security_group.go has NO special case for empty ingressRules/egressRules — the port group and the
base ACLs are created regardless. The empty body is the SAFE body, and a tenant cannot arrive at
"allow everything" by omission.

⚠ THE PORT ANNOTATION IS A COMMA-SEPARATED LIST OF GROUP NAMES, which is why "one group per purpose,
several groups per workload" is the substrate's own composition model and why this platform's schema
gives a single group the arity of one.

── The controller writes STATUS ONLY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

⚠ THE FIRST OBJECT IN THIS FAMILY FOR WHICH THAT IS TRUE. Every write pkg/controller/
security_group.go makes goes through patchSgStatus:

    Patch(ctx, sg.Name, types.MergePatchType, bytes, metav1.PatchOptions{}, "status")

— a merge patch against the "status" subresource, which the object declares with
+kubebuilder:subresource:status. There is no SecurityGroups().Update(...) of a spec anywhere in the
file. So the argument charts/managed/kube-ovn-vpc and charts/managed/kube-ovn-subnet use for
containment — "the controller rewrites .spec" — does NOT hold here, and NetworkSecurityGroups.Matches
is containment for the general reason instead.

── Validation the controller does, after the apply ───────────────────────────────────────────────

validateSgRule rejects: an IPVersion outside {ipv4, ipv6}; a priority outside
SecurityGroupPriorityMin..Max; a `pass` policy at the maximum tier; a malformed RemoteAddress or
LocalAddress; a RemoteSecurityGroup that does not exist; a port outside 1..65535 or min > max; and a
source port range without a LocalAddress.

⚠ EVERY ONE OF THOSE EXCEPT `min > max` IS ALSO REFUSED AT THIS PLATFORM'S API, by
PortRange.OptionalListPattern and Cidr.OptionalV4Pattern, before the write path answers 202. That is
the whole reason the port list is a patterned string rather than an array of numbers: bounds are
PROPERTY constraints on SchemaProperty and there is no per-element bounds member, so an array of
WholeNumber could carry no range check at all.

── Server-side apply: both rule arrays are ATOMIC, and here that is harmless ──────────────────────

Neither SecurityGroup.spec.ingressRules nor .egressRules carries an `x-kubernetes-list-type` or
`x-kubernetes-list-map-keys` marker, so both default to ATOMIC under SSA and the last applier owns
the whole list.

⚠ THAT IS FATAL FOR `routeTables` AND HARMLESS HERE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS WORTH STATING. Two
routeTables resources would have shared ONE Vpc's staticRoutes array and each would have converged by
erasing the other. One security group owns its whole SecurityGroup object, so there is no second
writer for atomicity to hurt.

── What this platform does not reach ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

`tier` (an operator's layering control, not a tenant's), `remoteSecurityGroup` (a cross-resource
reference with no reader — rule 2), `localAddress` and the source port range (per-rule refinements
this shape has no slot for), and `policy: drop` / `policy: pass` (only meaningful against a broader
allow at a higher priority, which is an arity this shape does not have). All are recorded in
conformance.yaml § owed.
